+**Subtopics:**
+*   **Coupled Strategy:** The inner integral dictates the initial step size for the outer integral. Assumes coherence between integrals. Reuses intermediate Simpson's values. Slightly more efficient. [S1]
+*   **Decoupled Strategy:** The outer step is controlled independently. The inner step only limits the integration interval. Theoretically better for high-frequency transfer function variations. Slightly more accurate. [S1]
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method avoids excessive re-computations by advancing step-by-step and reusing function evaluations shared by adjacent intervals, similar to caching in recursive Simpson's rule but with constant memory overhead per step [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Constant memory overhead per step, unlike recursive methods which use stack space proportional to recursion depth [S1].
-*   **Performance:** CPU architecture favors adaptive algorithms [S1]. GPU SIMD architecture favors Riemann summation because adaptive approaches require different step sizes per thread, reducing parallel efficiency [S1].
-*   **Accuracy:** Adaptive VRI offers better accuracy for similar or lower computational cost compared to Riemann summation, which requires prohibitive computational costs to reach similar accuracy with constant small step sizes [S1].
-*   **Coupled vs Decoupled:** Coupled strategy is slightly more efficient; Decoupled strategy is slightly more accurate [S1]. Both are equivalent in practice for most cases, but decoupled handles high-frequency transfer function variations better theoretically [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When accuracy control is required and CPU resources are available, or when GPU parallel efficiency can be managed (e.g., via divergence handling).
-*   **When Not to Use:** On GPUs where strict SIMD efficiency is prioritized over per-pixel accuracy, or when high-frequency details are not critical and uniform sampling suffices.
+*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** The method avoids excessive recomputations by advancing step-by-step and reusing function evaluations shared by adjacent intervals, similar to caching in recursive Simpson's rule but with constant memory overhead per step [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Tested on a 2.66 GHz Quad Core CPU and GeForce GTX 560 GPU. The CPU architecture favors adaptive algorithms, while the GPU SIMD architecture favors Riemann summation because adaptive approaches require different step sizes per thread, reducing parallel efficiency [S1].
+*   **Accuracy:** Offers better accuracy for similar or lower computational cost compared to Riemann summation with constant small step sizes, which requires prohibitive computational costs [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Accuracy control, efficiency through step size adaptation, GPU compatibility (via iterative approach).
+*   **Limitations:** Numerical error evaluation inaccuracies can cause some rays to exceed tolerance even with strict settings. High-frequency regions may be missed if the initial step size is too large or if the error evaluation sampling strategy fails. GPU parallel efficiency is lower than uniform sampling due to divergent step sizes [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When high accuracy is required and the scene has varying density/color features that benefit from adaptive sampling. Suitable for CPU rendering.
+*   **When not to use:** When maximum performance is required on GPU and uniform sampling is acceptable. When the scene has very high-frequency details that require very small step sizes regardless of adaptation.
 
 ## Background
-*   **Origin:** Proposed in "Accurate Volume Rendering based on Adaptive Numerical Integration" [S1].
-*   **History:** The method addresses the challenge of computing the Volume Rendering Integral (VRI) accurately while maintaining performance [S1]. It builds on adaptive Simpson's rule [S1].
-*   **Usage:** Tested on datasets from volvis.org (Bonsai, BluntFin, Teapot, Engine) [S1]. Hardware: 2.66 Ghz Quad Core CPU, GeForce GTX 560 GPU [S1].
+*   **Origin:** Proposed in "Accurate Volume Rendering based on Adaptive Numerical Integration" (2015) [S1].
+*   **History:** Builds on the Volume Rendering Integral (VRI) formulation by Engel [S1]. The adaptive Simpson's rule is based on work by Kuncir, McKeeman, and Lyness [S1].
+*   **Usage:** Tested on datasets from volvis.org (Bonsai, BluntFin, Teapot, Engine) [S1].
 
